### 6\. Interaction and motion specification

### 6.1 Motion principles

Use motion to explain state changes, not to decorate the screen.

| Interaction    | Proposed behaviour           |
| -------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Button press   | Scale feedback around 0.96   |
| Card selection | Smooth elevation and depth   |
| Wallet deck    | Spring-based movement        |
| Bottom sheet   | Drag-responsive expansion    |
| Navigation     | Consistent screen transition |
| Balance update | Numeric interpolation        |
| Voice          | Live waveform                |
| Scanner        | Processing feedback          |
| Modal close    | Smooth dismissal             |

### 6.2 Motion tokens

These are initial design targets, not fixed promises of 120 FPS or 120 Hz rendering.

| Motion token | Initial target                 |
| ------------ | ------------------------------ |
| Quick        | 120–160 ms                     |
| Standard     | 200–260 ms                     |
| Expressive   | 300–420 ms                     |
| Spring       | Tuned by stiffness and damping |
| Press scale  | 0.96                           |
| Drag         | Gesture-responsive             |

Actual animation performance must be tested on the target Android hardware.

### 6.3 Haptic feedback

* Light feedback for ordinary selection.

* Medium feedback for important confirmation.

* Distinct feedback for success or error where appropriate.

* No unnecessary vibration on every interaction.

* Respect Android system haptic preferences.

### 7\. Responsive and financial-data rules

This section is especially important because Ucred is a finance application.

### Currency formatting

Examples:

| Value        | Display |
| ------------ | ------- |
| ₹450         | ₹450    |
| ₹12,500      | ₹12.5K  |
| ₹1,25,000    | ₹1.25L  |
| ₹1,00,00,000 | ₹1Cr    |

The compact format is for overview cards only. Full precise values should remain accessible in transaction details and other relevant contexts.

Rules:

* Never allow currency values to wrap awkwardly.

* Avoid shrinking numbers until they become unreadable.

* Use proper Indian number grouping.

* Support large values and negative amounts.

* Keep currency symbol and amount visually connected.

* Test long customer names and unusually large values.

### Responsive layouts

Must support:

* Compact phones.

* Standard Android phones.

* Large phones.

* Landscape where relevant.

* System font scaling.

* Display scaling.

* Keyboard-visible states.

* Gesture navigation and system bars.

